outputs.exact_statement:
Let $X$ be a proper CAT(0) space and $n\ge2$ an integer. Every compactly supported $T\in\mathbf I_n(X)$ with $\partial T=0$ admits a compactly supported $S\in\mathbf I_{n+1}(X)$ such that $$\label{eq:main}
 \partial S=T,\qquad
 \mathbf M(S)\le c_n\mathbf M(T)^{(n+1)/n}
 =\frac{\mathbf M(T)^{(n+1)/n}}
 {(n+1)^{(n+1)/n}\omega_{n+1}^{1/n}}.$$ There is no restriction on the dimension of $X$ or on the integer multiplicities of the currents. The coefficient is optimal.

Let $M$ be a Cartan–Hadamard manifold of dimension $d\ge3$. For every bounded domain $\Omega\subset M$ with smooth boundary, $$\label{eq:classical}
 \operatorname{area}(\partial\Omega)
 \ge d\omega_d^{1/d}\operatorname{vol}(\Omega)^{(d-1)/d}.$$ The same inequality holds for relatively compact sets of finite perimeter, with ambient perimeter in place of boundary area.

For almost every density point $z\in E_i$, the seminorm in <a href="#eq:two-point-metric-differential" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:two-point-metric-differential">[eq:two-point-metric-differential]</a> is a Euclidean norm. Thus there is a measurable positive definite matrix $G_i(z)$ with $$\label{eq:chart-metric}
 N_z(w)^2=w^{\mathsf T}G_i(z)w,
 \qquad J_i(z)=\sqrt{\det G_i(z)}.$$ In particular, $$\label{eq:centered-metric-differential}
 d(\phi_i(z+w),\phi_i(z))=|w|_{G_i(z)}+o(|w|)
 \quad (z+w\in E_i).$$

Suppose that a finite-mass $d$-current $C$ has a representation by countably many determinant integrals with Lipschitz maps $\psi_i:F_i\to Y$, Borel sets $F_i\subset\mathbb R^d$, and real signed weights $\vartheta_i$. Suppose that, at almost every point carrying these weights, there is a measurable nonnegative quadratic form $P_i(z)$ such that $$\label{eq:quadratic-distance-majorant}
 d(\psi_i(z+w),\psi_i(z))^2
 \le P_i(z)[w,w]+o(|w|^2),\qquad z+w\in F_i.$$ If the measure $$\label{eq:quadratic-mass-majorant}
 \eta=\sum_i(\psi_i)_\#
       \bigl(|\vartheta_i|\sqrt{\det P_i}\,dz\bigr)$$ is finite, then $\|C\|\le\eta$. No normality of the separate chart currents is required.

For an integral $k$-current $C$ in $Y$, $$\label{eq:euclidean-lower-density}
 \liminf_{\rho\downarrow0}
       \rho^{-k}\|C\|(B(y,\rho))\ge\omega_k
 \qquad\text{for }\|C\|\text{-almost every }y.$$ The balls in this statement are open.

Let $Y$ be a compact CAT(0) space. Every integral two-cycle $T\in\mathbf I_2(Y)$ admits $S\in\mathbf I_3(Y)$ with $$\partial S=T,\qquad
 \mathbf M(S)\leq c_2\mathbf M(T)^{3/2}
          =\frac{\mathbf M(T)^{3/2}}{6\sqrt{\pi}}.$$

Let $Z$ be a nonempty compact metric space and let $k\ge1$. There is a countable collection $\mathcal T_k(Z)$ of finite lists $$\bigl((b_1,\pi^1),\ldots,(b_N,\pi^N)\bigr),$$ where the $b_j$ are Lipschitz, $\sum_j|b_j|\le1$ pointwise, and each $\pi^j$ is a $k$-tuple of $1$-Lipschitz functions, such that every $k$-current $C$ of finite mass on $Z$ satisfies $$\mathbf M(C)=\sup_{\mathcal T\in\mathcal T_k(Z)}
                  \sum_{(b,\pi)\in\mathcal T}C(b,\pi).
 \label{eq:countable-mass-tests}$$ The collection is independent of $C$.

For every nonnegative, radial nonincreasing, compactly supported Lipschitz function $f$ on $\mathbb R^n$, where $n>2$, $$ns_n^{2/n}
       \left(\int_{\mathbb R^n}f^p\,dx\right)^{2/p}
    \le \frac4{n-2}\int_{\mathbb R^n}|\nabla f|^2\,dx.
 \label{eq:radial-sobolev}$$

Fix $a>0$ and put $$w(t)=(1+at^2)^{-(n-2)/2}\qquad(t\in\mathbb R).$$ Then $vw(s)$ and $vw(s)^2$ belong to $\mathcal H$, and $$\label{chord:product-rules}
 \begin{split}
 D(vw(s))&=w(s)Dv+w'(s)\xi,\\
 D(vw(s)^2)&=w(s)^2Dv+2w(s)w'(s)\xi.
 \end{split}$$ If $f:\mathbb R\to[0,\infty)$ is smooth with bounded value and bounded first derivative, then $$\label{chord:radial-tests}
 g=\kappa^2v^{2+2\beta}f(s),\qquad
 \psi=\beta\kappa^2r^2v^{1+2\beta}f(s)$$ also belong to $\mathcal H$. In particular, $g$ is an admissible nonnegative test in the extended radial inequality, and $\psi$ is an admissible test in <a href="#eq:euler" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:euler">[eq:euler]</a>.

For every center $y\in Y$, with $r=d(y,\cdot)$, and every nonnegative $g\in\mathcal H$, $$\label{chord:completed-radial}
 \int_Y\bigl(ng\lvert Dr\rvert^2+r\langle Dr,Dg\rangle\bigr)\,d\mu
       \le\frac n4\int_Y r^2g\,d\mu.$$

For every center $y$ with $v(y)>0$, and every $a\ge0$, $$\label{eq:chord-transform}
 J_y(a)=\int_Y(1+as^2)^{-n}\,d\nu
                  \le(1+2aL_y)^{-n/2},$$ where $s$ and $L_y$ are defined in <a href="#chord:data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="chord:data">[chord:data]</a>.

For $\nu$-almost every center $y$, $$\label{chord:transform-density}
 \liminf_{a\to\infty}a^{n/2}J_y(a)
                         \ge\frac{s_n}{2^nW}.$$ Consequently, $$\label{eq:chord-upper}
 L_y\le2\left(\frac{W}{s_n}\right)^{2/n}<2
                    \qquad\text{for $\nu$-almost every }y.$$

Define $$\label{finish:weighted-moment}
 B_*:=\int_Y v^{p+2\beta}\,d\mu,
 \qquad
 I(y):=\int_Y d(y,x)^2v(x)^{p+2\beta}\,d\mu(x).$$ Then $0<B_*<\infty$, the function $I$ is finite and continuous on $Y$, and $$\label{finish:all-centers}
 W^2\le B_*I(y)\qquad\text{for every }y\in Y.$$ In particular, for the chord moments $$L_y=\int_Y
       \bigl[v(y)^\beta d(y,x)v(x)^\beta\bigr]^2\,d\nu(x),$$ one has $$\label{finish:opposite-average}
 \int_Y L_y\,d\nu(y)\ge2.$$
